Keep one ticket per evidenced cause, with an engineering owner and a count of
unique report IDs. A busy queue can contain different defects described in
similar words. Do not collapse them merely to shorten the queue.

## The rule

A report is a duplicate only when its evidence supports the same cause and
compatible product and affected path, following
[decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause). This replaces the subject-only
rule in the unchanged historical [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).

1. Retain the report ID, body, tenant, channel, and receipt time. Deduplicate
   overlapping export rows by ID.
2. Resolve the tenant's locale and booking-form version. For the date incident,
   use `causeOf` and `sameCause` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`, not browser locale or
   intake tags. Check the body against that classification.
3. If the cause and affected context agree, link to that cause's ticket. If they
   differ, keep separate tickets. If evidence is missing or contradictory, keep
   the report unplaced for investigation.
4. Preserve an audit trail when reclassifying. Count each report once and
   distinguish when it arrived from when the desk merged it.

<Callout>
Matching subjects are not evidence of a shared cause. The
[review of #4471](/kb/incidents/4471-review) links separate engineering issues and
records unexplained timing reports. Neither "known" nor "fix queued" proves a
production resolution.
</Callout>

## Try it

<DedupeExplorer />

## When you are unsure

Do not force a merge. Ask for the missing tenant, product, form version, or
reproduction, and retain contradictions for engineering. Continue routing
well-supported incoming reports while uncertain ones are investigated.

Confirm product scope before any broad remediation: this desk also serves
`sched-prod` and other products. Do not bulk-change live tickets or customer
invitations on the strength of an exported snapshot.
